    "reasoning": "Nitpicks: Minor formatting choices (bullets vs prose), including STD number alongside status, and extra explanatory commentary about reconciling sources are minor and don\u2019t materially reduce usefulness.\n\nCritical issues:\n1) Publication month/year is not reliably evidenced as coming from the RFC Editor record. The agent states \u201cPublication date: June 2022,\u201d but the provided screenshot of the RFC Editor \u201cAbout this RFC\u201d panel shows only the \u201cDate published\u201d label with the actual month/year not visible. That means we cannot verify the agent actually retrieved the publication month/year from the record; it could be a guess or memory.\n2) The task explicitly requires: \u201cDistinguish a fully obsoleted RFC from one for which only portions are obsoleted\u201d and \u201cUse the RFC Editor record rather than inferring relationships from another source.\u201d In the screenshots, the RFC Editor record\u2019s \u201cObsoletes (9)\u201d list does not show any partial/full qualifiers for any RFC. The agent nonetheless asserts a partial-vs-full breakdown (marking RFC 7230 as partial) based on the abstract/body text. Even if that partial claim is true in general, it does not satisfy the task constraint as written, because the partial/full distinction was not taken \u201cas indicated by the RFC Editor record.\u201d Moreover, the agent also simultaneously claims the RFC Editor record implies full obsolescence for all nine (because the list is unqualified) and then overrides that with the abstract\u2014this is internally inconsistent with the requirement to preserve what the RFC Editor record indicates.\n\nWhat the agent did accomplish: Screenshots confirm the agent accessed the RFC Editor site and the \u201cAbout this RFC\u201d panel, correctly captured title (HTTP Semantics), status (Internet Standard/STD 97), authors/editors (R. Fielding, M. Nottingham, J. Reschke), and correctly listed the nine RFCs shown under \u201cObsoletes (9)\u201d (2818, 7230, 7231, 7232, 7233, 7235, 7538, 7615, 7694).\n\nOutcome assessment: Because the user\u2019s request explicitly demands (a) publication month/year and (b) correct full-vs-partial obsolescence preserved per the RFC Editor record (not inferred), and the agent\u2019s response either cannot be verified from the record (publication date) and violates the constraint for partial-vs-full, the task is not successfully completed from the perspective of delivering the required authoritative metadata/relationships.",
